  "query_preview": "..Isolated to scattered showers with a few thunderstorms \nare ongoing as of this writing south and east of Tucson. However, \nthe storms for the most part are struggling today as some modest \nwarming/drying aloft is resulting in a bit of subsidence/capping, \nkeeping the vertical growth of the storms in check. There just isn't \nmuch in the way of larger scale lift or forcing to help things along \ntoday. However, with additional daytime heating, still can't rule \nout an isolated stronger storm or two through the afternoon with \nmain concern still being heavy rain and isolated flash flood threat. \nTucson will be on the western periphery of any activity with \nany storms diminishing fairly quickly this evening after sunset. \n\nAbove normal moisture levels will continue for early September \nSaturday through Monday with PWAT values mostly in the 1 to 1.5 inch \nrange across our forecast area with some modest assistance from \nHurricane Marie well southwest of Baja California. While moisture \nwill continue to remain elevated this weekend, there remain no well \ndefined triggers or lift to enhance showers and thunderstorms with \nmodest instability levels as mid/upper level ridge axis nudges into \nSE Arizona. Expect isolated to scattered showers and thunderstorms \neach day, mainly to the south and east of Tucson where there is \nbetter instability. Locally heavy rainfall will continue to be the \nmain threat. \n\nThereafter, from Tuesday and continuing through the rest of the work \nweek, it's shaping up to be a potentially more active period as a \nmore established lower to mid level southeasterly flow sets up with \npotential tropical influence from a possible system near southern \nBaja. Plenty of time to keep an eye on this but no sign of Monsoon \n2026 letting up as we head through the next week to 10 days. \n\nTemperatures will be within a couple of degrees of normal through \nWednesday, then dropping to below normal levels late next week.",
